var e=1,t=`econ-cycle-01`,n=`economics`,r=`The Business Cycle and What Works in Each Phase`,i=`Four phases, the order things turn in, and why almost every strategy's track record is really a record of the phases it lived through.`,a=`core`,o=[`econ-monetary-01`],s=9,c=[`CFA-L1`,`CAIA-L1`,`practical`],l=[{type:`concept`,id:`what-it-is`,body:`The [[business-cycle|business cycle]] is the repeated pattern of expansion and contraction in activity around the economy's trend. Four phases, though the boundaries are only clear afterwards:

**Early expansion.** Recovery from a trough. Spare capacity, falling unemployment, easy policy, rising confidence.

**Late expansion.** Capacity tightening, wages rising, policy turning restrictive, credit standards loose and getting looser.

**Slowdown.** Growth decelerating, earnings revisions turning down, credit spreads widening from very tight levels.

**Contraction.** A [[recession]]: falling output, rising unemployment, defaults, policy easing hard.

Cycles are not regular. Post-war expansions have run from one year to more than ten. Anyone claiming a cycle is "due" to end on age alone is describing a calendar, not an economy.`},{type:`concept`,id:`the-order`,body:`**The phases are less useful than the ORDER in which things turn. That order is remarkably stable even when the timing is not.**

Roughly, from earliest to latest:

**Financial conditions** move first — the [[yield-curve|curve]], credit spreads, equity prices. They are forecasts, which is exactly why they lead.

**Surveys and new orders** turn next. A [[purchasing-managers-index|purchasing managers' index]] asks firms about direction, so it turns before the activity it describes.

**Hard activity data** — production, retail sales, capital expenditure — follows.

**Employment** turns late. Firms cut hours before people, and hire back only once the recovery is convincing.

**Defaults and write-offs** turn last of all, well into the recovery, because a borrower fails after its revenue does.

**The investment consequence is direct: the data confirming a recession arrives after markets have bottomed, and the data confirming a boom arrives after markets have peaked.** Waiting for confirmation is systematically expensive, and it feels like prudence every time.`},{type:`intuition`,id:`track-records-are-cycles`,body:`Here is the point that makes this a diligence topic rather than an economics topic.

**A five-year track record is a record of a five-year stretch of the cycle.** A [[direct-lending|direct lending]] fund launched into a decade of falling defaults and cheap financing produced excellent numbers, and almost none of that was skill in the sense a selector cares about. A trend-following fund's flat decade was not incompetence — it was a decade without the sustained trends the strategy needs.

So the question is never "what did they return?" It is **"which phases has this strategy actually lived through, and what happened in the ones it has not?"**

Three specific traps follow.

**A strategy with no bad-phase data is untested, not proven.** Absence of a [[drawdown]] is absence of evidence.

**The strategies that look most attractive late in an expansion are usually the most cycle-dependent.** Levered credit, momentum, anything harvesting a spread that has been narrowing for years.

**A manager who explains their good years with skill and their bad years with the environment has told you they cannot tell the difference.** The honest version cites both.`},{type:`table`,id:`phase-table`,caption:`What tends to help and hurt, by phase`,headers:[`Phase`,`Tends to work`,`Tends to struggle`],rows:[[`Early expansion`,`Equity beta, cyclicals, [[distressed-debt|distressed]] positions taken at the trough, high-beta [[long-short-equity|long/short]]`,`Defensive positioning, cash, short books.`],[`Late expansion`,`[[carried-interest|Carry]]-harvesting strategies, [[merger-arbitrage|merger arbitrage]] on deal volume, levered credit`,`Value discipline, anything requiring cheap entry prices.`],[`Slowdown`,`Quality, duration, [[trend-following|trend following]] once direction establishes, [[global-macro|macro]]`,`Spread compression trades, high leverage, illiquid new commitments.`],[`Contraction`,`Government bonds, [[trend-following|trend]], [[dry-powder|dry powder]] deployed into dislocation`,`Levered credit, [[secondaries]] sellers, anything needing an exit window.`]]},{type:`example`,id:`worked-vintage`,body:`Two identical [[private-credit|private credit]] funds, same team, same underwriting standards. One deploys in a late expansion, the other deploys into a contraction eighteen months later.`,walkthrough:[`**Late-expansion fund.** Competition for deals is intense. Pricing is tight, so it lends at a spread of, say, 500bp. [[covenant|Covenants]] are loose because borrowers can choose their lender. Leverage on the underlying companies is high because everyone is paying up.`,`**Contraction fund.** Competition has vanished. It lends at 800bp with tight covenants, lower leverage and better documentation.`,`Same team. Same process. **Three hundred [[basis-point|basis points]] of spread and a materially better legal position, decided entirely by when the money was deployed.**`,`Now the bit that catches people out: the late-expansion fund will report better numbers *first*, because its loans are performing and nothing has defaulted yet. The contraction fund is deploying while headlines are terrible and its early marks may be poor.`,`**Read that carefully — the fund with the worse economics reports better results for the first two years.** By the time the ranking inverts, the allocation decision was made years earlier.`,`Sanity check worth reusing: **when comparing two private funds, compare deployment periods before comparing returns.** If the [[vintage-year|vintages]] differ, a return comparison is mostly a comparison of entry conditions.`]},{type:`concept`,id:`how-it-goes-wrong`,body:`**Phase-labelling after the fact.** Recessions are dated retrospectively, sometimes years later. Any claim to know the current phase in real time is a forecast wearing a label's clothing.

**Assuming the last cycle's playbook.** The 2010s rewarded duration, growth and leverage continuously. A process tuned on that decade is tuned on one regime.

**Ignoring the private markets lag.** Private valuations move quarters after public ones, so a private portfolio looks resilient in a drawdown and then catches up. The resilience is a reporting artefact and gets mistaken for diversification.

**Timing commitments.** Trying to deploy only into good vintages usually means committing late — after the dislocation has passed. Consistent commitment across vintages beats vintage timing for almost everyone, which is unsatisfying and well evidenced.

**Reading a single indicator as the cycle.** Every recession has a favourite indicator that called it, chosen after the fact from dozens that did not.`},{type:`pitfall`,id:`correlation-in-stress`,body:`**Diversification is measured in normal times and consumed in bad ones.**

Correlations between risk assets rise in a contraction. Strategies that looked independent for years — equity long/short, credit, real estate, [[private-equity|private equity]] — turn out to share one exposure: the price of risk. When that reprices, everything with a [[discount-rate|discount rate]] in it moves the same way.

This is not a reason to abandon diversification. It is a reason to distinguish two kinds: **diversification of return sources, which is real, and diversification of measured correlation, which is often an artefact of infrequent marks.**

The test is unglamorous and unavoidable: **what did each holding do in the worst quarter available, on the same dates?** Not average correlation over ten years — the same quarter, aligned. Most portfolios that look diversified on a correlation matrix look considerably less so under that test.`},{type:`onTheJob`,id:`on-the-job`,body:`**Ask which cycle phases the strategy has actually traded through.** A 2015 launch has no experience of a genuine credit contraction. That is a fact about the record, not an insult.

**Ask what happened in the worst quarter, and what the manager did about it.** The action taken matters more than the number, because the action reveals the process.

**Compare private funds by deployment period before comparing returns.** Different vintages mean different entry pricing, and the return gap is mostly that.

**Ask which phase the manager believes we are in and what would change their mind.** The second half is the real question — a view with no disconfirming evidence attached is a slogan.

**Be suspicious of asymmetric attribution.** Good years explained by skill and bad years by the environment is the clearest signal that a manager has not separated the two.`},{type:`keyTakeaways`,id:`takeaways`,items:[`The order things turn in is stable even though the timing is not: financial conditions, then surveys, then hard data, then employment, then defaults last.`,`Confirmation arrives after the turn — the data proving a recession comes out after markets have bottomed, which makes waiting for certainty systematically expensive.`,`A track record is a record of the cycle phases it lived through. Two identical private credit funds deploying eighteen months apart differed by 300bp of spread and a better legal position on identical underwriting.`,`Diversification is measured in calm and consumed in stress. Test it on aligned dates in the worst quarter, not on a ten-year average correlation.`]}],u=[{id:`econ-cycle-01-q1`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:2,tags:[`business-cycle`,`indicators`],concept:`the-order`,stem:`Which of these typically turns LAST in a cycle?`,choices:[`Credit spreads`,`Purchasing managers' surveys`,`Corporate default rates`,`Equity prices`],answerIndex:2,rationales:[`Spreads are a market price and therefore a forecast — they move early, often first.`,`Surveys ask about direction, so they turn before the activity they describe.`,`Correct. A borrower defaults after its revenue has already fallen and its cash has run out, so defaults keep rising well into a recovery.`,`Equity prices are forward-looking and typically bottom before the recession ends.`],explanation:`The practical consequence is that default rates peaking is a lagging confirmation of a recovery that markets priced months earlier. Buying distressed debt when defaults peak is buying at the point of maximum agreement, which is rarely the point of maximum value.`},{id:`econ-cycle-01-q2`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:2,tags:[`business-cycle`,`policy`],concept:`what-it-is`,stem:`In which phase is a central bank most likely to be tightening while growth still looks healthy?`,choices:[`Early expansion`,`Late expansion`,`Slowdown`,`Contraction`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Early expansion has spare capacity, so policy is still supportive.`,`Correct. Capacity is tight and wages are rising, so the bank restrains an economy that still looks strong on the surface — the phase where the data and the policy appear to contradict each other.`,`By the slowdown, tightening is usually finished and the debate has moved to when cuts start.`,`In a contraction the bank is easing.`],explanation:`This is the output gap from the GDP topic doing its work: the bank is responding to the level of capacity use, not the growth rate. It is also the phase in which the most dangerous investments get made, because financing is still cheap and confidence is highest.`},{id:`econ-cycle-01-q3`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`business-cycle`,`private-credit`],concept:`worked-vintage`,stem:`Two private credit funds with the same team deploy eighteen months apart, one late in an expansion and one in a contraction. Which reports better returns in its first two years, and which has the better economics?`,choices:[`The late-expansion fund on both counts`,`The late-expansion fund reports better early, but the contraction fund has better spreads, covenants and leverage`,`The contraction fund on both counts`,`They are indistinguishable, since the team and process are identical`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`It does report better early, because nothing has defaulted yet — but its loans were priced at tighter spreads with weaker covenants.`,`Correct. Early reported performance and underlying loan quality point in opposite directions here, and the reported figures resolve last.`,`Its economics are better and its early marks are usually worse, since it is deploying into falling prices and bad headlines.`,`Identical process, completely different entry conditions. The conditions are most of the outcome.`],explanation:`This inversion is why vintage discipline matters more than manager selection in private credit and buyout alike. It also explains a recurring allocation error: capital arrives after two good reported years, which by construction is late in the cycle that produced them.`},{id:`econ-cycle-01-q4`,type:`tfj`,difficulty:3,tags:[`business-cycle`,`misconception`],concept:`how-it-goes-wrong`,stem:`"This expansion has run for nine years, so a recession is due." True or false — and why?`,isTrue:!1,justifications:[`False — expansions do not die of age; they end from imbalances, shocks or policy tightening, and post-war durations have varied enormously`,`False — expansions last exactly as long as the credit cycle, which is ten years`,`True — long expansions are statistically more likely to end each year`,`False — because recessions are only identified retrospectively`],justificationIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. Duration alone carries very little information. What matters is what has built up — leverage, capacity pressure, a policy stance — not the number of years on the clock.`,`There is no fixed credit cycle length. The claim replaces one calendar with another.`,`This is the gambler's fallacy in macro dress, and it is the reasoning under test.`,`True but a different point: retrospective dating makes the phase hard to know, not the age-based claim wrong.`],explanation:`The useful reframing: instead of asking how old the expansion is, ask what would have to break. Naming the mechanism — a refinancing wall, a capacity constraint, an overtightening — gives you something observable to monitor, which an age-based claim never does.`},{id:`econ-cycle-01-q5`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`business-cycle`,`correlation`,`risk`],concept:`correlation-in-stress`,stem:`A portfolio shows low correlation between its private equity, private credit and real estate sleeves over ten years. What is the most likely explanation?`,choices:[`The three strategies genuinely have independent return drivers`,`Infrequent and manager-provided valuations smooth each series, which mechanically lowers measured correlation`,`The measurement period was too long`,`Private assets are inherently uncorrelated with each other`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`They share the price of risk, the cost of debt and exit conditions. Genuine independence between all three would be remarkable.`,`Correct. Quarterly marks set by the manager lag and smooth. Smoothing suppresses measured covariance, so low correlation is partly a statement about the data's frequency rather than about the economics.`,`A longer period generally gives a more reliable estimate — the problem is the inputs, not the window.`,`They are all exposed to financing conditions and valuation multiples, which is a shared driver by construction.`],explanation:`This is the same smoothing that flatters private volatility and beta. The correction is to test with aligned dates in a genuine stress quarter, or to compare against lagged public proxies. Both are less flattering and considerably more informative than the correlation matrix in the pitch.`},{id:`econ-cycle-01-q6`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`business-cycle`,`diligence`,`on-the-job`],concept:`track-records-are-cycles`,stem:`A manager attributes strong years to their process and weak years to the market environment. What does this most likely indicate?`,choices:[`Appropriate confidence in a repeatable process`,`That they have not separated skill from environment — and cannot tell you which drove either outcome`,`That the strategy is market-neutral`,`That the weak years were genuinely unrepresentative`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Confidence is not the issue. Asymmetric attribution is: it explains every outcome favourably, which means it explains nothing.`,`Correct. A manager who understands their own return drivers can name the environmental contribution to a good year too. One-directional attribution is a diagnostic, and it is one of the more reliable ones.`,`Market-neutral is a claim about exposures, and it would be testable — this is a claim about narrative.`,`Possibly true, and it cannot be established by a manager who only invokes the environment when it helps.`],explanation:`The follow-up worth asking: "how much of last year's return came from the environment?" A good manager has a number and a method. A weak one hears it as an accusation, which is itself informative.`},{id:`econ-cycle-01-q7`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`business-cycle`,`allocation`,`on-the-job`],concept:`how-it-goes-wrong`,stem:`An investment committee proposes pausing private market commitments until the cycle turns, then deploying into the dislocation. What is the strongest objection?`,choices:[`Dislocations never produce better returns`,`Turning points are only identifiable afterwards, so the pause usually extends past the dislocation and the money arrives late`,`Committing capital is always better than holding cash`,`Funds do not accept commitments during dislocations`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`They frequently do — the objection is not to the premise but to the ability to act on it.`,`Correct. The phase is knowable in hindsight and murky in real time, so a pause that intends to end at the trough tends to end after it, once conditions feel safe. That is the definition of arriving late.`,`Not true in general, and it is not the argument against vintage timing.`,`They very much do — capital is scarcest and most welcome precisely then.`],explanation:`The alternative is dull and effective: commit consistently across vintages so the portfolio owns every entry environment, and keep flexibility for opportunistic capital as an addition rather than as the plan. This is one of the few places where the boring institutional answer is also the evidenced one.`}],d=!1,f=null,p={schemaVersion:1,id:t,domain:n,title:r,summary:i,level:a,prereqs:o,estMinutes:9,examRelevance:c,lesson:l,questions:u,needsReview:!1,reviewNote:null};export{p as default,n as domain,s as estMinutes,c as examRelevance,t as id,l as lesson,a as level,d as needsReview,o as prereqs,u as questions,f as reviewNote,e as schemaVersion,i as summary,r as title};
